<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/14th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Kulikovo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1380">1380</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Kulikovo was fought on 8 September 1380 on Kulikovo Field near the upper Don, when an army of Russian principalities led by Grand Prince Dmitry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> defeated the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamai">Mamai</a>, the warlord of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>. Although <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a> sacked Moscow two years later, the victory earned Dmitry the name Donskoy and became a symbol of Russian resistance to Mongol rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-kulikovo.jpg" alt="Battle of Kulikovo" width="320" height="384" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Adolphe Yvon · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dmitry_Donskoy_in_the_Battle_of_Kulikovo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the Mongol invasion of Rus' in the 13th century the Russian principalities became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>. Moscow grew in power and often challenged its neighbours, and in 1300 it seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolomna">Kolomna</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryazan">Ryazan</a>. After the killing of Khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berdibeg">Berdi Beg</a> in 1359 the Golden Horde fell into what were called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-troubles">Great Troubles</a>, and the commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamai">Mamai</a>, Berdi Beg's son-in-law, took power in its western part and enthroned khans of his own. He was not a descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>, so his hold on power was tenuous, and he had to fight both for supremacy and against separatism while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, Moscow and Ryazan rose as new powers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In August 1380 Dmitry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> learned that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamai">Mamai</a>'s army was approaching. Mamai's envoys demanded a tribute raised to the level of the time of Khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jani-beg">Jani Beg</a>, and Dmitry agreed only to the amount of his earlier agreement. He gathered an army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolomna">Kolomna</a> and marched south, reaching the Don on 6 September. Jogaila of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> was marching to join Mamai. On the morning of 8 September, in thick fog, the Russian army crossed the Don.</p><p>The early sources give few details of the fighting. The fullest account, The Tale of the Rout of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamai">Mamai</a>, dates from the 16th century, and it is unknown whether it rests on an earlier source. It describes six regiments, with an ambush regiment hidden in an oak grove and Dmitry exchanging his coat and helmet with a boyar so as to fight unrecognised. The main forces clashed at about 10.35 in the morning, and Russian sources agree that a long and cruel melee followed in which many died on both sides. A third of the commanders of the banners were killed. Dmitry survived, wounded and exhausted, and was found among the corpses.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Jogaila</a> turned back on hearing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamai">Mamai</a>'s defeat. Dmitry made Prince Oleg of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryazan">Ryazan</a> accept him as his older brother. Mamai escaped to the Genoese stronghold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Caffa</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> and raised a new army, but without a legitimate khan his nobles went over to his rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a>, who gained full power and ended the twenty-year split of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>. Mamai fled to Caffa again and was killed.</p><p>The victory did not end Mongol domination, but it is traditionally regarded as the turning point at which Mongol influence began to fade and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>'s power to rise. Only five primary sources survive, none from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> side, yet the battle produced a larger body of medieval Rus' literature than any other event, the Kulikovo cycle, which includes the epic Zadonshchina.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Kulikovo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The army's weakness showed during the Great Troubles: in 1380 the warlord Mamai was defeated by Grand Prince Dmitry of Moscow at the <b>Battle of Kulikovo</b>, although Khan Tokhtamysh sacked Moscow two years later.</blockquote><a href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>On 8 September 1380 Dmitry led a coalition of Rus' princes to victory over the Tatar commander Mamai at the <b>Battle of Kulikovo</b> on the upper Don, but two years later Khan Tokhtamysh took and burned Moscow, and in 1408 the emir Edigu besieged it again.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Prince Fyodor Romanovich and his son were killed fighting for Dmitry Donskoy at <b>Kulikovo</b> in 1380.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-beloozero">Principality of Beloozero</a></li><li><blockquote>At <b>Kulikovo</b> in 1380 he and the commander Dmitry Bobrok led the ambush regiment whose attack decided the battle.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-serpukhov">Principality of Serpukhov</a></li></ul></section>
